WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.767
-Our first guest tonight is
a Tony- and Grammy Award-winning

00:00:03.767 --> 00:00:05.834
actor and Broadway legend
you know from shows

00:00:05.834 --> 00:00:07.701
such as "Gypsy" and "Evita."

00:00:07.701 --> 00:00:10.501
She's starring in the Broadway
revival of "Company,"

00:00:10.501 --> 00:00:12.868
which is now playing at
the Bernard B. Jacobs Theatre.

00:00:12.868 --> 00:00:15.701
Please welcome to the show
Patti LuPone, everyone!

00:00:15.701 --> 00:00:17.834
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:17.834 --> 00:00:26.100
♪♪

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:34.367
♪♪

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:35.634
-Hi!

00:00:35.634 --> 00:00:37.968
-Patti, I am so happy
you're here!

00:00:37.968 --> 00:00:40.934
-I'm so happy to be here.
I'm such a big fan of yours.

00:00:40.934 --> 00:00:44.634
And can I just say that
you and "A Closer Look"

00:00:44.634 --> 00:00:46.701
got me through the pandemic.

00:00:46.701 --> 00:00:49.334
The computer became
my best friend.

00:00:49.334 --> 00:00:53.300
I would find you, and you would
pinpoint the insanity of

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:56.934
this country with such great
humor that you leveled me.

00:00:56.934 --> 00:00:58.400
You kept me calm.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.133
You made me laugh, and for that,
I am indebted to you.

00:01:01.133 --> 00:01:02.567
-Oh, my goodness, thank you!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:07.133
What more could you ask for from
the beginning of an interview?

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:08.868
-It's true.
-That means the world to me

00:01:08.868 --> 00:01:11.100
because I've always been
such a huge fan of yours.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:15.100
And this is so thrilling
that you are on stage

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.267
in this revival of "Company."

00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:21.234
This is a very famous show,
a very famous role.

00:01:21.234 --> 00:01:24.100
And do you hesitate at all
when you get offered

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.534
something like this or do you
just jump in with both feet?

00:01:26.534 --> 00:01:30.868
-Well, I actually had given up
musicals when I did "War Paint"

00:01:30.868 --> 00:01:35.968
a couple of years ago because I
actually started performing on

00:01:35.968 --> 00:01:37.834
a bone-on-bone hip.

00:01:37.834 --> 00:01:41.968
I needed a hip replacement,
and we had just opened,

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:44.534
so I couldn't exactly
leave the show.

00:01:44.534 --> 00:01:47.467
And I -- Musicals are
difficult physically,

00:01:47.467 --> 00:01:49.567
and I have two new hips
and a new shoulder.

00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:50.367
-Oh, wow.
-Yeah.

00:01:50.367 --> 00:01:53.667
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:53.667 --> 00:01:55.567
And so I actually gave up
musicals,

00:01:55.567 --> 00:01:57.267
and then Marianne called me --
Marianne Elliott --

00:01:57.267 --> 00:01:58.734
who's the director of "Company,"

00:01:58.734 --> 00:02:00.434
was the director of "War Horse,"
the director of

00:02:00.434 --> 00:02:02.200
"Curious Incident of
the Dog in the Night-Time."

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.067
And I had put it out in the
universe that I wanted to

00:02:04.067 --> 00:02:06.567
work with this woman
when I saw both of those shows.

00:02:06.567 --> 00:02:08.601
I gave up musicals, she called.

00:02:08.601 --> 00:02:10.367
[ Laughter ]
I said, "No, no, no, no, no."

00:02:10.367 --> 00:02:13.133
And then I thought, "If I don't
work with her,

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:15.267
if I don't say yes,
she'll never ask me again."

00:02:15.267 --> 00:02:17.033
So I said yes,
and I went to London

00:02:17.033 --> 00:02:19.300
to do the production in London,
this production in London.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:20.934
And then of course
I was gonna do it

00:02:20.934 --> 00:02:24.834
in my native state, my hometown.

00:02:24.834 --> 00:02:27.834
-And then it was supposed to
open right --

00:02:27.834 --> 00:02:30.567
like March, I think, 20th
of 2020.

00:02:30.567 --> 00:02:31.968
-On Steve's birthday.

00:02:31.968 --> 00:02:34.167
-Yeah, on Stephen Sondheim's
birthday, it was gonna open.

00:02:34.167 --> 00:02:36.100
And then, obviously,
the pandemic hit,

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:39.834
and then we very sadly lost
Stephen last month.

00:02:39.834 --> 00:02:41.701
You knew him very well.

00:02:41.701 --> 00:02:44.667
-I-I-I knew him.
[ Laughs ]

00:02:44.667 --> 00:02:46.534
I don't think anybody --
-It's so interesting.

00:02:46.534 --> 00:02:48.400
So you don't know
if anybody really knew him.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.400
-Well, I think his intimates
knew him.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:51.534
I think Jeff knew him very well,

00:02:51.534 --> 00:02:53.400
and I know Mia knows him
very well.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.000
That would be Mia Farrow.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.734
And he has a coterie of friends.

00:02:57.734 --> 00:03:00.300
And I have a working
relationship with him,

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:02.434
and I did have a social
relationship with him

00:03:02.434 --> 00:03:05.367
because we both live in
the same county as you do.

00:03:05.367 --> 00:03:08.267
And there would be high teas,

00:03:08.267 --> 00:03:11.234
there would be New Year's Eve
parties, there would be dinners,

00:03:11.234 --> 00:03:13.767
but I was never comfortable,
I have to say.

00:03:13.767 --> 00:03:15.300
He's an intimidating presence.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:20.901
He's so smart and scary,
so I never felt comfortable.

00:03:20.901 --> 00:03:24.100
-How was he -- Obviously,
you were in a situation

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:26.000
where he could give you notes,

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.167
he could give you praise,
he could give you criticism.

00:03:28.167 --> 00:03:32.667
How was he when he would present
that feedback to a performer?

00:03:32.667 --> 00:03:35.934
-Well, in my particular case,
it would depend on the show

00:03:35.934 --> 00:03:37.868
and it would depend on --

00:03:37.868 --> 00:03:40.534
For instance, I have this
incredible note that sits on

00:03:40.534 --> 00:03:43.467
my dressing room mirror
about this show.

00:03:43.467 --> 00:03:46.968
And when we did "A Little Night
Music" at Ravinia,

00:03:46.968 --> 00:03:51.467
he came backstage with really
succinct notes and praise.

00:03:51.467 --> 00:03:54.234
But then when I did Fosca
at Lincoln Center Live,

00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:58.801
I, um, was, um, uh,
destroyed by a note.

00:03:58.801 --> 00:03:59.968
[ Laughter ]

00:03:59.968 --> 00:04:01.367
-He gave you a note
that destroyed you?

00:04:01.367 --> 00:04:03.367
-Totally.
I didn't understand it,

00:04:03.367 --> 00:04:05.901
and all I heard was
monotonous mush.

00:04:05.901 --> 00:04:08.033
And I also thought,
if there was anybody

00:04:08.033 --> 00:04:10.300
with less experience than me,
they would have turned in

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:12.334
their Equity cards 'cause it was
a dressing-down

00:04:12.334 --> 00:04:16.701
that was so shocking that -
I mean, I stopped listening

00:04:16.701 --> 00:04:18.534
because I was sort of
freaked out.

00:04:18.534 --> 00:04:20.901
And then we figured out
what the note was.

00:04:20.901 --> 00:04:23.434
I asked a bunch of people
backstage what we thought

00:04:23.434 --> 00:04:25.300
the note would have been,
and then, the next day,

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:27.834
he came to my dressing room
that night when it was live

00:04:27.834 --> 00:04:30.033
at Lincoln Center, and he said,
"Night and day."

00:04:30.033 --> 00:04:31.667
And so I knew that I had
gotten his approval

00:04:31.667 --> 00:04:33.067
and I had accomplished the note.

00:04:33.067 --> 00:04:34.934
And basically, he couldn't
understand me

00:04:34.934 --> 00:04:36.234
when I was emotional.

00:04:36.234 --> 00:04:38.200
So that's a note
that's easy to give,

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:39.801
as opposed to what I heard.

00:04:39.801 --> 00:04:41.300
-Right.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:44.767
Well, I've worked with people
who are like that.

00:04:44.767 --> 00:04:47.934
You know, Lorne Michaels is
someone who is also a genius,

00:04:47.934 --> 00:04:50.701
and his notes are very cryptic
to the point that he tells them

00:04:50.701 --> 00:04:52.267
to you, you're destroyed,

00:04:52.267 --> 00:04:54.767
and then you also don't
quite know what he said.

00:04:54.767 --> 00:04:56.234
-Yeah, right, exactly.

00:04:56.234 --> 00:04:59.567
Yes, I think they get caught up
in themselves or...

00:04:59.567 --> 00:05:00.734
-Yeah, I think, maybe.

00:05:00.734 --> 00:05:02.501
[ Laughter ]

00:05:02.501 --> 00:05:04.267
I think maybe you're right.

00:05:04.267 --> 00:05:06.100
[ Laughter ]

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:07.567
-I don't know whether
I'm allowed to say that,

00:05:07.567 --> 00:05:09.267
but it's true.

00:05:09.267 --> 00:05:11.033
They have a point to make,

00:05:11.033 --> 00:05:13.400
and they're passionate
about the point,

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:16.601
but they're not necessarily
diplomatic.

00:05:16.601 --> 00:05:19.467
-Yeah.
-But I think it's true of

00:05:19.467 --> 00:05:22.300
any of us that are
in the lower echelon.

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:24.834
The actors are the low man
on the theatrical totem pole,

00:05:24.834 --> 00:05:26.234
so we get beat up all the time.

00:05:26.234 --> 00:05:28.100
-Yeah.
-It's just the way it goes.

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:32.767
It's not nice, but we have to
expect it from our director,

00:05:32.767 --> 00:05:35.334
from the musical director
if you're in a musical,

00:05:35.334 --> 00:05:36.801
from the composer,
from the lyricist.

00:05:36.801 --> 00:05:40.334
You're going to get notes that
are -- mm, change your life.

00:05:40.334 --> 00:05:41.601
[ Laughter ]

00:05:41.601 --> 00:05:43.968
-Being away from
a live audience,

00:05:43.968 --> 00:05:45.734
what is it like having it back?

00:05:45.734 --> 00:05:48.000
Is it everything you remembered
or is it even more valuable

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:49.434
having taken time away?

00:05:49.434 --> 00:05:51.601
-How did you feel?
How did you feel?

00:05:51.601 --> 00:05:53.267
-You know, it's great.
-It is.

00:05:53.267 --> 00:05:55.100
-It is really great.
I also think --

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:57.901
[ Cheers and applause ]
Yeah.

00:05:57.901 --> 00:06:01.267
I think I forgot how easy it was

00:06:01.267 --> 00:06:02.901
to pander like that
and get applause.

00:06:02.901 --> 00:06:06.267
[ Laughter ]

00:06:06.267 --> 00:06:08.567
-Uh...
[ Laughter ]

00:06:08.567 --> 00:06:12.067
But I think that it was
a scary time

00:06:12.067 --> 00:06:14.868
because we didn't know
when it would end.

00:06:14.868 --> 00:06:16.000
-Yeah.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:17.567
-That was the thing that was
freaking me out.

00:06:17.567 --> 00:06:19.467
I went to the dark side
so many times.

00:06:19.467 --> 00:06:21.200
I thought, "Is this my life now?

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:23.868
And if this is my life,
do I want this life?"

00:06:23.868 --> 00:06:25.634
-Yeah.
-You know what I mean?

00:06:25.634 --> 00:06:27.033
"Is this retirement?"

00:06:27.033 --> 00:06:31.667
But I think, also, the audiences
that we are playing to are happy

00:06:31.667 --> 00:06:35.267
and grateful that there is
that live interaction again.

00:06:35.267 --> 00:06:37.234
We all missed it.

00:06:37.234 --> 00:06:40.534
I mean, just how many Netflix
shows can we binge-watch?

00:06:40.534 --> 00:06:41.701
-Right.
-Do you know what I mean?

00:06:41.701 --> 00:06:43.801
Without some sort of connection?

00:06:43.801 --> 00:06:48.601
And I find that in "Company"
now, I feel the same response

00:06:48.601 --> 00:06:50.000
I'm sure that you do
with this audience.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:52.167
-It's really lovely.
-It's really --

00:06:52.167 --> 00:06:55.100
I could cry right now because we
need that interaction.

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:56.934
We need that connection with
each other.

00:06:56.934 --> 00:06:59.100
-You did something else during
the pandemic that I want to

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:00.968
thank you for, because it was
interesting, right?

00:07:00.968 --> 00:07:02.834
We didn't have audiences,
but yet we also --

00:07:02.834 --> 00:07:04.100
I don't know if you felt this.

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:06.434
I felt this connection
with my audience

00:07:06.434 --> 00:07:08.968
that I hadn't had before,
because I was doing a show alone

00:07:08.968 --> 00:07:11.434
into an iPad,
hyperaware that most people

00:07:11.434 --> 00:07:13.234
watch it alone into an iPad.
-Right.

00:07:13.234 --> 00:07:15.234
-And so even though
there was nobody

00:07:15.234 --> 00:07:17.467
and I couldn't hear anything, I
felt like they were right there

00:07:17.467 --> 00:07:18.367
and there was an authenticity
to that.

00:07:18.367 --> 00:07:19.467
-I was! I was!

00:07:19.467 --> 00:07:21.067
-Yeah, and I'm so happy
to hear that.

00:07:21.067 --> 00:07:22.801
I felt you.

00:07:22.801 --> 00:07:25.200
But you started shooting videos

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:27.133
in your basement
that were the same thing.

00:07:27.133 --> 00:07:28.834
Like, it was an authentic
version of you

00:07:28.834 --> 00:07:31.267
that I feel like maybe people
like me,

00:07:31.267 --> 00:07:32.434
who are a huge fan of you,

00:07:32.434 --> 00:07:33.667
maybe we didn't know
your personality as well,

00:07:33.667 --> 00:07:34.767
and then you shot these videos

00:07:34.767 --> 00:07:36.133
and we got to know you
through that.

00:07:36.133 --> 00:07:37.634
Even though we were
a million miles away.

00:07:37.634 --> 00:07:39.400
What gave you the idea to start
shooting those?

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:41.267
-Rosie O'Donnell.
We -- You know, a lot of us,

00:07:41.267 --> 00:07:43.734
I think, had became more famous
on Zoom

00:07:43.734 --> 00:07:46.167
than I'd ever been
in my professional life.

00:07:46.167 --> 00:07:48.567
Rosie O'Donnell did a benefit
for the Actors Fund,

00:07:48.567 --> 00:07:49.934
and she asked me to sing.

00:07:49.934 --> 00:07:51.968
I was going to do it a cappella,

00:07:51.968 --> 00:07:53.868
but I had to get the note
from the piano,

00:07:53.868 --> 00:07:55.200
and the piano is downstairs,

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:57.501
and the piano is right opposite
the pinball machine.

00:07:57.501 --> 00:07:59.667
And people saw
the pinball machine

00:07:59.667 --> 00:08:01.901
and they wanted to see the rest
of the basement.

00:08:01.901 --> 00:08:03.234
And I said to my husband,

00:08:03.234 --> 00:08:05.267
"Well, let's show them
the rest of the basement.

00:08:05.267 --> 00:08:07.334
What the hell?
What have we got to lose?"

00:08:07.334 --> 00:08:11.868
And so we started to show them
the basement.

00:08:11.868 --> 00:08:13.133
That's what it was,
the basement tape.

00:08:13.133 --> 00:08:15.634
-The basement was one of
the stars of the pandemic.

00:08:15.634 --> 00:08:17.501
And for anybody who missed it,

00:08:17.501 --> 00:08:20.033
here's a quick look at
Patti's basement.

00:08:20.033 --> 00:08:22.934
-I already made a video,
and it won't download,

00:08:22.934 --> 00:08:26.033
so now I'm just wildly dancing.

00:08:27.501 --> 00:08:30.200
[ Clacking ]
That's the pinball machine!

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:32.100
Want to see it?
Oh, my God, here we go!

00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:34.100
It's my ball.
-No, it's not.

00:08:34.100 --> 00:08:35.167
-Hey, oh [bleep]

00:08:35.167 --> 00:08:36.501
[Bleep] [bleep]

00:08:36.501 --> 00:08:38.400
The video I just sent
was pretty great,

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:40.400
and I can't download it
because it's too long.

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:42.567
What's with Twitter?
What the [bleep] with Twitter?

00:08:42.567 --> 00:08:43.834
Never mind. Okay.

00:08:43.834 --> 00:08:45.234
I'm gonna show you this
and then we'll end it

00:08:45.234 --> 00:08:47.133
because I have no time.

00:08:47.133 --> 00:08:49.067
So, this is --
this is something --

00:08:49.067 --> 00:08:50.434
I'm very proud of this.

00:08:50.434 --> 00:08:53.100
This is the Second Stage
All-Star 1987.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:56.033
Second Stage, second prize for
most time spent in the gutter.

00:08:56.033 --> 00:08:57.601
Who won first? Okay.

00:08:57.601 --> 00:08:59.100
Okay, I got to stop. Bye!

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:00.434
[ Laughter ]

00:09:00.434 --> 00:09:02.667
-It's quite a basement.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:02.667 --> 00:09:05.801
Quite a basement.

00:09:05.801 --> 00:09:07.567
-My husband has
a huge collection.

00:09:07.567 --> 00:09:09.534
-Yeah, he's put together --
It's wonderful.

00:09:09.534 --> 00:09:10.567
Hey, thank you so much
for being here.

00:09:10.567 --> 00:09:11.934
-Thank you for having me.
-Just a delight.

00:09:11.934 --> 00:09:13.601
You guys, that's Patti LuPone!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:13.601 --> 00:09:17.267
"Company" is on Broadway now at
the Bernard B. Jacobs Theatre.

00:09:17.267 --> 00:09:18.601
For tickets
and more information,

00:09:18.601 --> 00:09:21.467
please visit
www.companymusical.com.

